ML_PAGE("l14-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l14\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 14 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain the mechanism, apply it to the scenario, then evaluate and conclude. Each mock question below has its full model answer behind a reveal; the complete subjective notes are underneath.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l14\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>The four-move answer (10-20 mark questions)</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. Establish the contract and the position</b><p>Say who has the <em>right</em> and who has the <em>obligation</em>: call or put, buyer or writer, then the underlying, strike, expiry, premium, multiplier and settlement assumptions.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Explain the mechanism</b><p>Separate <b>payoff</b> (expiry value before the premium) from <b>profit</b> (after the premium and any stock position). Show why the writer is paid up front.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. Calculate and analyse</b><p>Write each leg's cash flow in a small table: option payoff, premium, any share profit, then the total, per share first and per contract second. Premium appears once.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. Evaluate and conclude</b><p>Name the maximum loss, the break-even and what the position does <em>not</em> protect against (costs, mismatch, expiry). Link the choice to the investor's goal and give a conditional conclusion.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"card-note\">Chapter 14 answers lose marks in three places: payoff mixed up with profit, a missing multiplier or premium, and judging a strategy from the premium alone (\"it pays me\", \"it costs so little\").</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box span-all\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p><b>Q:</b> A stock is at $\\text{RM}53$ at expiry. A call with strike $\\text{RM}50$ was bought for $\\text{RM}5$ per share. Did the buyer profit?</p>\n        <p>\"The call is in the money, so the buyer made a profit.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">States the moneyness correctly but equates it with profit; no payoff, no premium, no number.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"If the share is $\\text{RM}53$ at expiration and the strike is $\\text{RM}50$, the call payoff is $\\text{RM}3$ per share. The buyer paid $\\text{RM}5$ per share, so the net result is a loss of $\\text{RM}2$ per share, or $\\text{RM}200$ on a $100$-share contract. The call is in the money, but its payoff has not recovered the premium: the break-even is $50 + 5 = \\text{RM}55$. Exercising or selling still beats letting the option lapse, because it reduces the loss from $\\text{RM}500$ to $\\text{RM}200$. The matched writer gains $\\text{RM}200$ before costs.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-good\">Right and payoff, premium, profit, break-even, a justified decision, and the writer's side.</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><div class=\"card tut-link-card\"><h3>Tutorial questions for this chapter</h3><p>7 questions from the course tutorials test this chapter, each with an exam-ready answer.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"primary-btn\" data-tut-open=\"14\">Open the Chapter 14 tutorial questions</button></div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Mock examination: questions with model answers</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><span>Contract rights, moneyness and profit (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A stock trades today at RM52. A call with strike RM50 costs RM5 per share; a put with strike RM55 costs RM4 per share. Each covers 100 shares and has the same expiration. Consider each contract separately.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Explain the rights and obligations of the buyer and writer of each option, and calculate each buyer's upfront contract premium.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Calculate each option's current intrinsic value and time value per share, and state its moneyness.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] If the stock closes at RM53 at expiration, calculate the call buyer's and put buyer's contract payoffs and profits. Explain why moneyness does not guarantee a profit.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [4] Calculate the corresponding writer's option-only profit for each contract, and identify each buyer's expiration break-even stock price.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Rights and premiums [6].</strong> A call buyer has the right to buy the underlying at the strike under the contract terms; the call writer is obligated to sell if assigned. A put buyer has the right to sell at the strike; the put writer is obligated to buy if assigned. The option holder may let the right expire. The call buyer pays $5\\times100=\\mathbf{RM500}$; the put buyer pays $4\\times100=\\mathbf{RM400}$. <em>Marking: call rights/obligations 2, put rights/obligations 2, premiums 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Moneyness [5].</strong> Call intrinsic value $=\\max(52-50,0)=RM2$ per share; time value $=5-2=RM3$. Put intrinsic value $=\\max(55-52,0)=RM3$ per share; time value $=4-3=RM1$. Both are in the money because their intrinsic values are positive. At the money means the stock and strike are equal; out of the money means the option has zero intrinsic value under these definitions. <em>Marking: each intrinsic/time pair 2, moneyness 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Expiration [5].</strong> At RM53, call payoff $=\\max(53-50,0)\\times100=RM300$ and profit $=300-500=\\mathbf{-RM200}$. Put payoff $=\\max(55-53,0)\\times100=RM200$ and profit $=200-400=\\mathbf{-RM200}$. Both have positive exercise value, but neither payoff recovers its upfront premium. <em>Marking: call payoff/profit 2, put payoff/profit 2, distinction 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Writer and break-even [4].</strong> Under the matched, frictionless option-only assumptions each writer gains RM200 when the corresponding buyer loses RM200. The call buyer breaks even at $50+5=\\mathbf{RM55}$; the put buyer at $55-4=\\mathbf{RM51}$. Actual counterparties' fees and other positions can make their total returns differ. <em>Marking: writer results 2, break-even prices 2.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><span>Leverage and writer exposure (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor may buy 100 shares at RM50 each or buy one RM50-strike call for RM5 per share, covering 100 shares. Consider two expiration outcomes: RM75 and RM45. There are no dividends or costs.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Calculate the initial stock cost, call cost and stock buyer's profit and percentage return if the stock rises to RM75.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Calculate the call buyer's payoff, profit and percentage return in the RM75 scenario. Explain the leverage comparison.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Calculate the stock and call buyers' profits and percentage returns if the stock instead ends at RM45.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Compare the loss exposure of a naked call writer and an uncovered put writer with RM50 strike and RM5 premium, assuming the stock cannot fall below zero. Discuss why the writer's premium is not free income.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Share purchase [5].</strong> Stock cost $=100\\times50=\\mathbf{RM5,000}$; call premium $=100\\times5=\\mathbf{RM500}$. At RM75, stock profit $=100(75-50)=RM2,500$, a $2,500/5,000=\\mathbf{50\\%}$ return. <em>Marking: stock cost 1, call cost 1, stock profit 1, stock return 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Call [5].</strong> At RM75, call payoff $=100\\max(75-50,0)=RM2,500$; profit $=2,500-500=\\mathbf{RM2,000}$. Return on premium $=2,000/500=\\mathbf{400\\%}$. The same 100-share exposure to the favorable price move is achieved with a smaller initial premium, magnifying the percentage return; this does not make the positions equivalent in risk or ownership. <em>Marking: payoff 1, profit 1, percentage 2, interpretation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Adverse move [5].</strong> At RM45, stock profit $=100(45-50)=-RM500$, or <strong>-10%</strong> of RM5,000. The call expires with zero payoff, so its buyer loses RM500, or <strong>-100%</strong> of the premium. The call buyer's maximum option-only loss is the paid premium, but expiration limits the time available for a favorable move. <em>Marking: stock result 2, call result 2, explanation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Writing risks [5].</strong> An uncovered call writer's option-only profit is $100[5-\\max(S_T-50,0)]$; as the stock rises without a theoretical upper limit, loss is theoretically unlimited. An uncovered put writer's profit is $100[5-\\max(50-S_T,0)]$; assuming the stock cannot fall below zero, maximum loss is $(50-5)\\times100=\\mathbf{RM4,500}$. The premium is the writer's maximum option-only profit, but the contingent obligations can produce much larger losses. Margin, assignment and actual costs matter. <em>Marking: naked-call exposure 2, put bound 2, premium/risk interpretation 1.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><span>Hedging a long and a short position (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Investor A buys 100 shares for RM50 each and simultaneously buys one RM45-strike put for RM3 per share. Investor B sells 100 shares short at RM50 each and simultaneously buys one RM55-strike call for RM3 per share. Calculate each position from its initiation; ignore short-sale borrowing costs, collateral interest, dividends and early exercise.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate A's total profit if the stock ends at RM35 and if it ends at RM65. Show the stock and put components separately.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Calculate B's total profit at the same two expiration prices, showing the short-stock and call components.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Determine the maximum expiration loss for each position under the stated assumptions and explain how the hedge works.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [4] Discuss two limitations or costs of these hedges and explain why owning an option differs from owning the shares.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Long stock plus put [6].</strong> At RM35, stock loss $=100(35-50)=-RM1,500$. Put payoff $=100(45-35)=RM1,000$ and put premium $=-RM300$, so total $-1,500+1,000-300=\\mathbf{-RM800}$. At RM65, stock gain is RM1,500, the put expires worthless, and total $1,500-300=\\mathbf{RM1,200}$. <em>Marking: two stock results 2, put payoffs/premium 2, totals 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Short stock plus call [5].</strong> At RM35, short-stock gain $=100(50-35)=RM1,500$; call payoff is zero and premium is RM300, giving <strong>RM1,200</strong> profit. At RM65, short-stock loss is $100(50-65)=-RM1,500$; call payoff is $100(65-55)=RM1,000$, so total is <strong>-RM800</strong> after the RM300 premium. <em>Marking: short-stock components 2, option components 2, totals 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Maximum loss [5].</strong> For A, below RM45 the put's gains offset further stock losses: $100(45-50-3)=\\mathbf{-RM800}$. For B, above RM55 the call's gains offset further losses on the short position: $100(50-55-3)=\\mathbf{-RM800}$. The protection applies to the stated matched quantity and expiration calculation; the premium is part of each result. <em>Marking: A floor 2, B cap 2, mechanism 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Limitations [4].</strong> The premium reduces returns if the option expires without being exercised. The protection ends at expiration and can be incomplete if the amount, dates or underlying do not match; actual assignment, trading, borrowing and other costs can matter. An option holder has contractual rights, not ordinary shareholder voting or dividend rights merely from owning the option. <em>Marking: two limitations 2, ownership distinction 2.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><span>Covered-call writing (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor buys 100 shares at RM50 each and writes one call with strike RM55, receiving RM2 per share. At expiration the share price may be RM40, RM55 or RM60. Assume the shares cover the writer's obligation and the call is exercised when its payoff is positive.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate total expiration profit for each stock price, explicitly combining share profit, premium and call obligation.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Determine the maximum profit, break-even stock price and maximum possible loss, assuming the share price cannot be negative.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Explain how the premium changes the downside and how the written call changes upside compared with owning the shares alone.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [4] Evaluate: \"A covered call eliminates investment risk because the writer receives a premium.\"</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Expiration profits [6].</strong> Stock profit is $100(S_T-50)$, premium received RM200, and the written-call payoff is $-100\\max(S_T-55,0)$.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Expiration stock price</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Stock profit</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Call payoff plus premium</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Combined profit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RM40</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">-RM1,000</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM200 - RM0</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>-RM800</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM55</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM500</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM200 - RM0</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>RM700</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM60</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM1,000</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM200 - RM500</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>RM700</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Marking: 2 marks for each correctly assembled outcome.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Bounds [5].</strong> Maximum profit is $100(55-50+2)=\\mathbf{RM700}$ at RM55 or above. Below the strike, break-even is $50-2=\\mathbf{RM48}$. If the stock falls to zero, maximum loss is $100(0-50+2)=\\mathbf{-RM4,800}$, assuming no negative share price and no other cash flows. <em>Marking: maximum profit 2, break-even 1, maximum loss 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Trade-off [5].</strong> The RM2 premium provides a limited buffer against losses; it does not prevent a large stock decline. When the stock rises beyond RM55, call obligation offsets additional share gains, capping total profit at RM700. Owning stock alone would retain upside but receive no option premium. <em>Marking: downside buffer 2, capped upside 2, comparison 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation [4].</strong> Reject the claim: at RM40 the position loses RM800, and at zero it loses RM4,800 under the stated assumptions. \"Covered\" means the stock is available to meet the call obligation; it does not protect the stock's value. Actual costs and assignment timing can affect results. <em>Marking: conclusion 1, quantitative evidence 1, coverage/risk mechanism 2.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><span>Vertical spread and long straddle (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>An investor constructs a bull call spread by buying one RM50-strike call for RM6 per share and writing one RM60-strike call for RM2 per share, both on the same stock with the same expiration and 100-share multiplier. Separately, another investor buys a RM50-strike call for RM4 and a RM50-strike put for RM3, with the same expiration and multiplier.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Calculate the spread's initial net debit and profits if the stock expires at RM45, RM55 or RM70.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [4] Determine the spread's maximum profit, maximum loss and break-even price at expiration.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [6] Calculate the long straddle's initial cost and profits if the stock expires at RM40, RM50 or RM65. Determine both break-even prices.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Compare the price movement each strategy benefits from and explain why a large move does not guarantee a profit if it occurs too late or if assumptions differ.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Bull call spread [5].</strong> Net debit is $(6-2)\\times100=\\mathbf{RM400}$. Profit is $100[\\max(S_T-50,0)-\\max(S_T-60,0)-4]$.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stock at expiration</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Long call payoff</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Short call payoff</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Net profit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RM45</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM0</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM0</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>-RM400</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM55</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM500</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM0</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>RM100</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM70</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM2,000</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">-RM1,000</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>RM600</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Marking: debit 1, each outcome 1, formula or component clarity 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Spread bounds [4].</strong> Maximum profit is $(60-50-4)\\times100=\\mathbf{RM600}$ at RM60 or above. Maximum loss is the <strong>RM400</strong> net debit at RM50 or below. Break-even is $50+4=\\mathbf{RM54}$. <em>Marking: max profit 1.5, max loss 1, break-even 1.5.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Long straddle [6].</strong> Total premium is $(4+3)\\times100=\\mathbf{RM700}$. Profit is $100[\\max(S_T-50,0)+\\max(50-S_T,0)-7]$. At RM40, put payoff is RM1,000 and profit <strong>RM300</strong>; at RM50, both payoffs are zero and loss <strong>RM700</strong>; at RM65, call payoff is RM1,500 and profit <strong>RM800</strong>. Break-even prices are $50-7=\\mathbf{RM43}$ and $50+7=\\mathbf{RM57}$. <em>Marking: cost 1, three outcomes 3, two break-evens 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Comparison [5].</strong> The bull call spread benefits from an upward move but has capped profit beyond RM60; the short call helps finance the purchase. The long straddle benefits at expiration from a sufficiently large move either side of RM50, but loses its premium if the stock ends at the strike. A move after expiration cannot rescue an expired position. Before expiration, time value, volatility and market prices matter, so expiration break-evens do not by themselves predict an early-sale result. <em>Marking: spread 1, straddle 1, timing 1, before-expiry valuation and qualification 2.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><span>Index and currency hedges (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Part A: A portfolio worth RM100,000 follows a stock index currently at 1,000 closely for this one-period example. The investor buys <strong>10 cash-settled index puts</strong>, each with strike 950, quoted premium 20 index points and a multiplier of RM10 per index point. At expiration the index is 900 and the portfolio, before the option, falls 10% to RM90,000. Use only the stated final prices and ignore other costs.</p>\n<p>Part B: Separately, a Malaysian exporter expects USD10,000 at expiration. The current exchange rate is RM4.50 per USD. The exporter buys a cash-settled USD put with strike RM4.40 per USD and premium RM0.05 per USD, covering the full USD10,000. The rate at expiration is RM4.20 per USD.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Calculate the index-put premium, expiration payoff and option profit for all 10 contracts.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Calculate the combined portfolio-and-hedge ending value and profit relative to the initial portfolio plus option cost. Explain why this hedge is not a complete guarantee.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [6] Calculate the exporter's ringgit receipts without the option, option premium, option payoff and net receipts after the hedge.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [4] Explain why an index hedge can differ from a single-stock hedge, and state two contract terms to verify before using an index or currency option. Briefly explain what a longer-dated option such as a LEAPS-style contract changes and does not guarantee.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<hr /><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Index put [5].</strong> Premium $=10\\times20\\text{ points}\\times RM10/\\text{point}=\\mathbf{RM2,000}$. Expiration payoff $=10\\times(950-900)\\times RM10=\\mathbf{RM5,000}$. Option profit $=5,000-2,000=\\mathbf{RM3,000}$. <em>Marking: premium 1, payoff 2, profit 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Combined result [5].</strong> The portfolio is worth RM90,000 and the option settles for RM5,000, so combined ending value is <strong>RM95,000</strong>. Initial outlay is RM100,000 plus RM2,000 premium, or RM102,000; combined profit is $95,000-102,000=\\mathbf{-RM7,000}$. The put protects only below the 950 strike in this example and costs RM2,000; mismatch between the index and actual portfolio, contract size, timing or other costs can reduce protection. <em>Marking: ending value 1, initial amount 1, loss 1, protection limitations 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Currency put [6].</strong> Unhedged exporter receipts $=USD10,000\\times RM4.20=\\mathbf{RM42,000}$. Premium $=USD10,000\\times RM0.05=\\mathbf{RM500}$. Put payoff $=USD10,000\\times(4.40-4.20)=\\mathbf{RM2,000}$. Net hedged receipts are $42,000+2,000-500=\\mathbf{RM43,500}$. The cash-settlement assumption allows the exporter to convert receipts at the market rate and add the option settlement. <em>Marking: 1.5 each for receipts, premium, payoff and net result.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Scope and terms [4].</strong> An index option references a broad index, so a portfolio with different holdings or sensitivity may not move one-for-one with it; a single-stock option references a specified security. Verify underlying and quote units, contract multiplier, expiration, exercise style and settlement method. A longer-dated option gives a longer period before expiration, but generally has an upfront cost and does not guarantee a profitable or complete hedge. <em>Marking: index-versus-stock mismatch 1, two terms 2, longer-dated qualification 1.</em></p>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l14\" id=\"l14s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">6 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"How to answer an options question\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l14s-s1\" title=\"How to answer an options question\">How to answer: position first</button></li><li data-full=\"Mock subjective examination\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l14s-s2\" title=\"Mock subjective examination\">Mock exam: six 20-mark questions</button></li><li data-full=\"Full model answers and illustrative marking guidance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l14s-s3\" title=\"Full model answers and illustrative marking guidance\">Model answers and marking guidance</button></li><li data-full=\"Short-answer revision\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l14s-s4\" title=\"Short-answer revision\">Eight short-answer prompts</button></li><li data-full=\"Formula and decision guide\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l14s-s5\" title=\"Formula and decision guide\">Formula and decision guide</button></li><li data-full=\"Final integrated exercise\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l14s-s6\" title=\"Final integrated exercise\">Final exercise: \"free\" protection</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l14\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Chapter 14 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>6 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 14 - Subjective Exam Preparation</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l14s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">How to answer an options question</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>An option question is a contract and a position, not just a prediction about a share price. Identify <strong>who has the right or obligation, the underlying, strike, expiration, premium, contract multiplier and settlement assumptions</strong>. Separate payoff from profit: payoff is the expiration value before premium; profit accounts for the premium and any associated stock position.</p>\n<p>A weak answer says, \"The call is in the money, so the buyer made a profit.\" A stronger answer says, \"If the share is RM53 at expiration and the strike is RM50, the call payoff is RM3 per share. If the buyer paid RM5 per share, the net result is a RM2 loss per share. The call is in the money, but its payoff has not recovered the premium.\"</p>\n<p>The following six cases, marks and model answers are original practice material, not an official exam scheme. Attempt the questions before reading the separate answers. For all stock-option cases, assume <strong>one contract covers 100 shares</strong>, premiums are quoted per share, options are held to expiration, and the investor can exercise or receive an economically equivalent settlement at the stated expiration price. Profit is measured from the stated position's initiation and excludes taxes, commissions, financing, dividends and early exercise unless explicitly stated. These are hypothetical contract terms; actual contract sizes, exercise style and settlement vary.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l14s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Mock subjective examination</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Question 1 - Contract rights, moneyness and profit (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A stock trades today at RM52. A call with strike RM50 costs RM5 per share; a put with strike RM55 costs RM4 per share. Each covers 100 shares and has the same expiration. Consider each contract separately.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Explain the rights and obligations of the buyer and writer of each option, and calculate each buyer's upfront contract premium.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Calculate each option's current intrinsic value and time value per share, and state its moneyness.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] If the stock closes at RM53 at expiration, calculate the call buyer's and put buyer's contract payoffs and profits. Explain why moneyness does not guarantee a profit.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [4] Calculate the corresponding writer's option-only profit for each contract, and identify each buyer's expiration break-even stock price.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 2 - Leverage and writer exposure (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investor may buy 100 shares at RM50 each or buy one RM50-strike call for RM5 per share, covering 100 shares. Consider two expiration outcomes: RM75 and RM45. There are no dividends or costs.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Calculate the initial stock cost, call cost and stock buyer's profit and percentage return if the stock rises to RM75.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Calculate the call buyer's payoff, profit and percentage return in the RM75 scenario. Explain the leverage comparison.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Calculate the stock and call buyers' profits and percentage returns if the stock instead ends at RM45.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Compare the loss exposure of a naked call writer and an uncovered put writer with RM50 strike and RM5 premium, assuming the stock cannot fall below zero. Discuss why the writer's premium is not free income.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 3 - Hedging a long and a short position (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>Investor A buys 100 shares for RM50 each and simultaneously buys one RM45-strike put for RM3 per share. Investor B sells 100 shares short at RM50 each and simultaneously buys one RM55-strike call for RM3 per share. Calculate each position from its initiation; ignore short-sale borrowing costs, collateral interest, dividends and early exercise.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate A's total profit if the stock ends at RM35 and if it ends at RM65. Show the stock and put components separately.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Calculate B's total profit at the same two expiration prices, showing the short-stock and call components.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Determine the maximum expiration loss for each position under the stated assumptions and explain how the hedge works.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [4] Discuss two limitations or costs of these hedges and explain why owning an option differs from owning the shares.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 4 - Covered-call writing (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investor buys 100 shares at RM50 each and writes one call with strike RM55, receiving RM2 per share. At expiration the share price may be RM40, RM55 or RM60. Assume the shares cover the writer's obligation and the call is exercised when its payoff is positive.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate total expiration profit for each stock price, explicitly combining share profit, premium and call obligation.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Determine the maximum profit, break-even stock price and maximum possible loss, assuming the share price cannot be negative.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Explain how the premium changes the downside and how the written call changes upside compared with owning the shares alone.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [4] Evaluate: \"A covered call eliminates investment risk because the writer receives a premium.\"</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 5 - Vertical spread and long straddle (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>An investor constructs a bull call spread by buying one RM50-strike call for RM6 per share and writing one RM60-strike call for RM2 per share, both on the same stock with the same expiration and 100-share multiplier. Separately, another investor buys a RM50-strike call for RM4 and a RM50-strike put for RM3, with the same expiration and multiplier.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Calculate the spread's initial net debit and profits if the stock expires at RM45, RM55 or RM70.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [4] Determine the spread's maximum profit, maximum loss and break-even price at expiration.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [6] Calculate the long straddle's initial cost and profits if the stock expires at RM40, RM50 or RM65. Determine both break-even prices.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Compare the price movement each strategy benefits from and explain why a large move does not guarantee a profit if it occurs too late or if assumptions differ.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 6 - Index and currency hedges (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>Part A: A portfolio worth RM100,000 follows a stock index currently at 1,000 closely for this one-period example. The investor buys <strong>10 cash-settled index puts</strong>, each with strike 950, quoted premium 20 index points and a multiplier of RM10 per index point. At expiration the index is 900 and the portfolio, before the option, falls 10% to RM90,000. Use only the stated final prices and ignore other costs.</p>\n<p>Part B: Separately, a Malaysian exporter expects USD10,000 at expiration. The current exchange rate is RM4.50 per USD. The exporter buys a cash-settled USD put with strike RM4.40 per USD and premium RM0.05 per USD, covering the full USD10,000. The rate at expiration is RM4.20 per USD.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Calculate the index-put premium, expiration payoff and option profit for all 10 contracts.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Calculate the combined portfolio-and-hedge ending value and profit relative to the initial portfolio plus option cost. Explain why this hedge is not a complete guarantee.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [6] Calculate the exporter's ringgit receipts without the option, option premium, option payoff and net receipts after the hedge.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [4] Explain why an index hedge can differ from a single-stock hedge, and state two contract terms to verify before using an index or currency option. Briefly explain what a longer-dated option such as a LEAPS-style contract changes and does not guarantee.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l14s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Full model answers and illustrative marking guidance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Answer 1 - Rights and value components</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Rights and premiums [6].</strong> A call buyer has the right to buy the underlying at the strike under the contract terms; the call writer is obligated to sell if assigned. A put buyer has the right to sell at the strike; the put writer is obligated to buy if assigned. The option holder may let the right expire. The call buyer pays $5\\times100=\\mathbf{RM500}$; the put buyer pays $4\\times100=\\mathbf{RM400}$. <em>Marking: call rights/obligations 2, put rights/obligations 2, premiums 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Moneyness [5].</strong> Call intrinsic value $=\\max(52-50,0)=RM2$ per share; time value $=5-2=RM3$. Put intrinsic value $=\\max(55-52,0)=RM3$ per share; time value $=4-3=RM1$. Both are in the money because their intrinsic values are positive. At the money means the stock and strike are equal; out of the money means the option has zero intrinsic value under these definitions. <em>Marking: each intrinsic/time pair 2, moneyness 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Expiration [5].</strong> At RM53, call payoff $=\\max(53-50,0)\\times100=RM300$ and profit $=300-500=\\mathbf{-RM200}$. Put payoff $=\\max(55-53,0)\\times100=RM200$ and profit $=200-400=\\mathbf{-RM200}$. Both have positive exercise value, but neither payoff recovers its upfront premium. <em>Marking: call payoff/profit 2, put payoff/profit 2, distinction 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Writer and break-even [4].</strong> Under the matched, frictionless option-only assumptions each writer gains RM200 when the corresponding buyer loses RM200. The call buyer breaks even at $50+5=\\mathbf{RM55}$; the put buyer at $55-4=\\mathbf{RM51}$. Actual counterparties' fees and other positions can make their total returns differ. <em>Marking: writer results 2, break-even prices 2.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 2 - Leveraged exposure and writer risk</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Share purchase [5].</strong> Stock cost $=100\\times50=\\mathbf{RM5,000}$; call premium $=100\\times5=\\mathbf{RM500}$. At RM75, stock profit $=100(75-50)=RM2,500$, a $2,500/5,000=\\mathbf{50\\%}$ return. <em>Marking: stock cost 1, call cost 1, stock profit 1, stock return 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Call [5].</strong> At RM75, call payoff $=100\\max(75-50,0)=RM2,500$; profit $=2,500-500=\\mathbf{RM2,000}$. Return on premium $=2,000/500=\\mathbf{400\\%}$. The same 100-share exposure to the favorable price move is achieved with a smaller initial premium, magnifying the percentage return; this does not make the positions equivalent in risk or ownership. <em>Marking: payoff 1, profit 1, percentage 2, interpretation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Adverse move [5].</strong> At RM45, stock profit $=100(45-50)=-RM500$, or <strong>-10%</strong> of RM5,000. The call expires with zero payoff, so its buyer loses RM500, or <strong>-100%</strong> of the premium. The call buyer's maximum option-only loss is the paid premium, but expiration limits the time available for a favorable move. <em>Marking: stock result 2, call result 2, explanation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Writing risks [5].</strong> An uncovered call writer's option-only profit is $100[5-\\max(S_T-50,0)]$; as the stock rises without a theoretical upper limit, loss is theoretically unlimited. An uncovered put writer's profit is $100[5-\\max(50-S_T,0)]$; assuming the stock cannot fall below zero, maximum loss is $(50-5)\\times100=\\mathbf{RM4,500}$. The premium is the writer's maximum option-only profit, but the contingent obligations can produce much larger losses. Margin, assignment and actual costs matter. <em>Marking: naked-call exposure 2, put bound 2, premium/risk interpretation 1.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 3 - Protective put and short stock plus call</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Long stock plus put [6].</strong> At RM35, stock loss $=100(35-50)=-RM1,500$. Put payoff $=100(45-35)=RM1,000$ and put premium $=-RM300$, so total $-1,500+1,000-300=\\mathbf{-RM800}$. At RM65, stock gain is RM1,500, the put expires worthless, and total $1,500-300=\\mathbf{RM1,200}$. <em>Marking: two stock results 2, put payoffs/premium 2, totals 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Short stock plus call [5].</strong> At RM35, short-stock gain $=100(50-35)=RM1,500$; call payoff is zero and premium is RM300, giving <strong>RM1,200</strong> profit. At RM65, short-stock loss is $100(50-65)=-RM1,500$; call payoff is $100(65-55)=RM1,000$, so total is <strong>-RM800</strong> after the RM300 premium. <em>Marking: short-stock components 2, option components 2, totals 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Maximum loss [5].</strong> For A, below RM45 the put's gains offset further stock losses: $100(45-50-3)=\\mathbf{-RM800}$. For B, above RM55 the call's gains offset further losses on the short position: $100(50-55-3)=\\mathbf{-RM800}$. The protection applies to the stated matched quantity and expiration calculation; the premium is part of each result. <em>Marking: A floor 2, B cap 2, mechanism 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Limitations [4].</strong> The premium reduces returns if the option expires without being exercised. The protection ends at expiration and can be incomplete if the amount, dates or underlying do not match; actual assignment, trading, borrowing and other costs can matter. An option holder has contractual rights, not ordinary shareholder voting or dividend rights merely from owning the option. <em>Marking: two limitations 2, ownership distinction 2.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 4 - Covered call</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Expiration profits [6].</strong> Stock profit is $100(S_T-50)$, premium received RM200, and the written-call payoff is $-100\\max(S_T-55,0)$.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Expiration stock price</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Stock profit</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Call payoff plus premium</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Combined profit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RM40</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">-RM1,000</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM200 - RM0</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>-RM800</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM55</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM500</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM200 - RM0</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>RM700</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM60</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM1,000</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM200 - RM500</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>RM700</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Marking: 2 marks for each correctly assembled outcome.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Bounds [5].</strong> Maximum profit is $100(55-50+2)=\\mathbf{RM700}$ at RM55 or above. Below the strike, break-even is $50-2=\\mathbf{RM48}$. If the stock falls to zero, maximum loss is $100(0-50+2)=\\mathbf{-RM4,800}$, assuming no negative share price and no other cash flows. <em>Marking: maximum profit 2, break-even 1, maximum loss 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Trade-off [5].</strong> The RM2 premium provides a limited buffer against losses; it does not prevent a large stock decline. When the stock rises beyond RM55, call obligation offsets additional share gains, capping total profit at RM700. Owning stock alone would retain upside but receive no option premium. <em>Marking: downside buffer 2, capped upside 2, comparison 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation [4].</strong> Reject the claim: at RM40 the position loses RM800, and at zero it loses RM4,800 under the stated assumptions. \"Covered\" means the stock is available to meet the call obligation; it does not protect the stock's value. Actual costs and assignment timing can affect results. <em>Marking: conclusion 1, quantitative evidence 1, coverage/risk mechanism 2.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 5 - Spread and straddle</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Bull call spread [5].</strong> Net debit is $(6-2)\\times100=\\mathbf{RM400}$. Profit is $100[\\max(S_T-50,0)-\\max(S_T-60,0)-4]$.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stock at expiration</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Long call payoff</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Short call payoff</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Net profit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>RM45</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM0</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM0</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>-RM400</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM55</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM500</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM0</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>RM100</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RM70</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">RM2,000</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">-RM1,000</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"><strong>RM600</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Marking: debit 1, each outcome 1, formula or component clarity 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Spread bounds [4].</strong> Maximum profit is $(60-50-4)\\times100=\\mathbf{RM600}$ at RM60 or above. Maximum loss is the <strong>RM400</strong> net debit at RM50 or below. Break-even is $50+4=\\mathbf{RM54}$. <em>Marking: max profit 1.5, max loss 1, break-even 1.5.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Long straddle [6].</strong> Total premium is $(4+3)\\times100=\\mathbf{RM700}$. Profit is $100[\\max(S_T-50,0)+\\max(50-S_T,0)-7]$. At RM40, put payoff is RM1,000 and profit <strong>RM300</strong>; at RM50, both payoffs are zero and loss <strong>RM700</strong>; at RM65, call payoff is RM1,500 and profit <strong>RM800</strong>. Break-even prices are $50-7=\\mathbf{RM43}$ and $50+7=\\mathbf{RM57}$. <em>Marking: cost 1, three outcomes 3, two break-evens 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Comparison [5].</strong> The bull call spread benefits from an upward move but has capped profit beyond RM60; the short call helps finance the purchase. The long straddle benefits at expiration from a sufficiently large move either side of RM50, but loses its premium if the stock ends at the strike. A move after expiration cannot rescue an expired position. Before expiration, time value, volatility and market prices matter, so expiration break-evens do not by themselves predict an early-sale result. <em>Marking: spread 1, straddle 1, timing 1, before-expiry valuation and qualification 2.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 6 - Index and currency hedges</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Index put [5].</strong> Premium $=10\\times20\\text{ points}\\times RM10/\\text{point}=\\mathbf{RM2,000}$. Expiration payoff $=10\\times(950-900)\\times RM10=\\mathbf{RM5,000}$. Option profit $=5,000-2,000=\\mathbf{RM3,000}$. <em>Marking: premium 1, payoff 2, profit 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Combined result [5].</strong> The portfolio is worth RM90,000 and the option settles for RM5,000, so combined ending value is <strong>RM95,000</strong>. Initial outlay is RM100,000 plus RM2,000 premium, or RM102,000; combined profit is $95,000-102,000=\\mathbf{-RM7,000}$. The put protects only below the 950 strike in this example and costs RM2,000; mismatch between the index and actual portfolio, contract size, timing or other costs can reduce protection. <em>Marking: ending value 1, initial amount 1, loss 1, protection limitations 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Currency put [6].</strong> Unhedged exporter receipts $=USD10,000\\times RM4.20=\\mathbf{RM42,000}$. Premium $=USD10,000\\times RM0.05=\\mathbf{RM500}$. Put payoff $=USD10,000\\times(4.40-4.20)=\\mathbf{RM2,000}$. Net hedged receipts are $42,000+2,000-500=\\mathbf{RM43,500}$. The cash-settlement assumption allows the exporter to convert receipts at the market rate and add the option settlement. <em>Marking: 1.5 each for receipts, premium, payoff and net result.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Scope and terms [4].</strong> An index option references a broad index, so a portfolio with different holdings or sensitivity may not move one-for-one with it; a single-stock option references a specified security. Verify underlying and quote units, contract multiplier, expiration, exercise style and settlement method. A longer-dated option gives a longer period before expiration, but generally has an upfront cost and does not guarantee a profitable or complete hedge. <em>Marking: index-versus-stock mismatch 1, two terms 2, longer-dated qualification 1.</em></p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l14s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Short-answer revision</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><ol>\n<li>Why can an option be in the money and still produce a loss for its buyer?</li>\n<li>What is the difference between an option's intrinsic value and its time value before expiration?</li>\n<li>How do a buyer's right and a writer's obligation differ?</li>\n<li>Why can a call buyer lose all the premium while a stock buyer loses less than 100% of the investment?</li>\n<li>What exposure remains after writing a covered call?</li>\n<li>How does a protective put change the payoff of a stock position, and what does it cost?</li>\n<li>How does a bull call spread cap both its potential gain and its loss?</li>\n<li>Why can an index put fail to fully protect an individual portfolio?</li>\n</ol></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l14s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Formula and decision guide</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l14_payoff\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Payoff and profit at expiry</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Pick one of the four positions and move the strike, premium and expiry price; the solid line is profit after the premium, the dashed line is the payoff before it, and the break-even is marked.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Let $S_T$ be the underlying price at expiration, $K$ the strike, $c$ the call premium, $p$ the put premium, and $N$ the specified share or contract multiplier. Premiums below are per share; multiply the entire per-share result by $N$.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Position or quantity</th>\n<th>Expiration formula</th>\n<th>What to check</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Call intrinsic value</td>\n<td>$\\max(S-K,0)$</td>\n<td>Use current $S$ before expiration; positive intrinsic value is not necessarily profit.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Put intrinsic value</td>\n<td>$\\max(K-S,0)$</td>\n<td>Intrinsic value cannot be negative.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time value</td>\n<td>Option premium - current intrinsic value</td>\n<td>Quoted price and intrinsic value must use the same units.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long call profit</td>\n<td>$N[\\max(S_T-K,0)-c]$</td>\n<td>Break-even at $K+c$.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long put profit</td>\n<td>$N[\\max(K-S_T,0)-p]$</td>\n<td>Break-even at $K-p$, where attainable.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Short call or put profit</td>\n<td>Premium received minus corresponding option payoff</td>\n<td>An uncovered call's theoretical loss is unbounded; a put on a nonnegative stock has a finite maximum loss.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long shares plus put</td>\n<td>$N[(S_T-S_0)+\\max(K-S_T,0)-p]$</td>\n<td>Protection and cost apply to the matched quantity through expiration.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Covered call</td>\n<td>$N[(S_T-S_0)+c-\\max(S_T-K,0)]$</td>\n<td>Premium buffers some downside; written call caps upside.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Index or currency option</td>\n<td>Intrinsic payoff x stated point or currency multiplier - premium</td>\n<td>Contract settlement, quotation units and hedge exposure matter.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l14s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Final integrated exercise</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>An investor holds a concentrated stock position and wants both protection against a sharp fall and unlimited participation in a rally, with no cost. Compare a protective put, covered call and a put funded partly by writing a call. A strong answer shows how each changes the combined payoff, the premium received or paid, the time horizon and the maximum upside or downside under specified terms. Explain the trade-off rather than treating protection as free, and assess the remaining concentration and counterparty or contract risks.</p>\n<p><em>Source note:</em> Based on <code>app/pdf/</code> (Pearson, 2014). Questions, prices, contract assumptions and marking guidance are original. Several slide examples require correction: slide 14-17's 100 shares at <span class=\"cur\">$</span>50 cost <strong><span class=\"cur\">$</span>5,000</strong>, not <span class=\"cur\">$</span>50,000, making a <span class=\"cur\">$</span>2,500 stock gain <strong>50%</strong>, not 5%; slide 14-18's writer earns <strong>positive <span class=\"cur\">$</span>500</strong> when the option expires worthless; and slide 14-41's unbounded-loss statement must distinguish an uncovered call from a short put on a stock that cannot fall below zero. The visual table on slide 14-33 labels an out-of-the-money call's underlying difference as negative; its option intrinsic value is <strong>zero</strong>, not negative. Slide 14-42's covered-call table contains inconsistent strike and break-even labels, so use the explicitly defined example above. Historical option-market descriptions and contract specifications should not be treated as current universal rules.</p></div></details></div></details>");
